  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nToday through tomorrow night...\n\nRecycled monsoonal moisture is continuing to stream up over the \nregion out of the southeast around the high positioned over the \ndesert southwest. Given the amount of instability and abundance of \nmoisture, there will be around a 40 to 50 percent chance of \nafternoon showers and thunderstorms developing, with the San \nBernardinos mostly at play. Some of these storms will be capable of \nproducing rainfall rates of 0.50 inches per hour. Expect that some \nof these storms will also produce strong and gusty winds due to \noutflows and high DCAPE values exceeding 1000 J/kg. There could also \nbe some small hail associated with these storms. This will continue \ninto the evening hours, with storms beginning to move towards the \nnortheast by later in the day as mid-level flow begins to shift to \nbeing more out of a southwesterly direction. There may also be \nsome isolated areas of flash flooding as a result of the storms, \nas they may rapidly intensity along the mountain/desert interface,\nespecially where there are currently some SBCAPE values of 4000 \nJ/kg, with temperatures/dews of low 90s over low 70s within the \nCoachella Valley. These showers and storms will likely make their \nway into the high deserts with skies continuing to clear as these \nshowers/storms dissipate and move out of the CWA. The marine layer\nwill be rather dispersed and then fill back in by late into the \nnight. As troughing continues to influence the region from \nupstream, with should allow bases to slightly rise into the \nmorning hours tomorrow.\n\nThursday through Wednesday...\n\nTroughing from upstream will continue to influence the region with \ngradually cooling temperatures through the end of the week, and \ntemperatures becoming as much as 10 degrees below the seasonal \naverage for some areas. This will also increase surface gusty winds, \nespecially during the afternoon for the high deserts, and mountain \ncanyons/passes by later in the week, with gusts approaching 60 mph \nwithin Gorgonio Pass. There will also become more persistence with \nonshore flow, with the marine layer becoming more prevalent \novernight and into the morning hours. Going into early next week, \nensembles reflect a breakdown of the troughing over the E Pacific\nwaters and thus a return to more seasonal temperatures, while \nremaining dry.",
